# Retain opposing worlds across operating policies

Version `0.1.0`, 18 September 2026. Status `exploratory`.

Publication correction: the first CSV at `5611b53` serialized the 4,088
anchor thresholds as dictionary text. They now use exact `numerator/denominator`
strings. Case outcomes, bounds, identities and private scientific results are
unchanged. Both CSV digests are retained in [summary.json](summary.json);
the first published version remains in Git history.

All **110 targeted one-edit bound gaps now have checked worlds on opposite
sides of strict improvement**. The finite search uses the existing 64-image
development operands at their already retained score thresholds. Every one
of the parent study's 7,707 decisions, universal bounds, memberships and
blocked inputs remains unchanged. This is stronger witness evidence for
unresolved comparisons, not an observation or cost advantage.

The [parent study](../../operating-policy/0.1.0/README.md) measured every
retained operating cell using original extremal worlds and deletion worlds.
That bank did not necessarily contain an extremal insertion or replacement
for a newly filtered prediction graph. The disclosed [follow-up plan](PLAN.md)
selects all remaining one-edit gaps before the new search: 10 primary-cell
rows and 100 diagnostic-anchor rows. The allocation spans 185 distinct
filtered image states; 87 already attain their bounds and 98 need search.

The original exact-rational finite construction is reused on those 98 states.
It tests 92,658 candidate rectangles, 956 distinct matching neighborhoods and
9,539 neighborhood/removal pairs, retaining 196 checked endpoint proofs.
There are 146 improved component endpoints. All 110 changed case-evidence
labels belong to the declared target allocation; no other row changes.
No state hits a cap or fails. The original gap rows remain in the parent
packet, with before/after evidence in [all results](results.csv).

